/**
 * Digging and placing blocks on a planet — CODE-SPLIT.
 *
 * A REGISTERED CHUNK MANIFEST MODULE (MANIFEST_MODULES and ALLOWLIST in
 * tooling/check-shader-imports.mjs): the import site of `MaterialPluginBase`
 * for the cut a predicted dig makes, loaded only by dynamic `import()` from
 * the planet view, for a planet whose description offers editing. A planet
 * that does not never fetches it.
 *
 * What it does, about the visitor, every frame:
 * - fetches the nodes of the chunks within reach (planetEdit.ts) and
 *   points at the block under the crosshair (editPick.ts), outlined;
 * - digs and places on the world's keys (`planet.edit-keys`, by default the
 *   primary and secondary buttons), while the view holds the pointer;
 * - shows each change at once: a placed block is drawn as its own box in its
 *   icon, and a dug block is cut out of the blocks drawn, by a material
 *   plugin that discards what lies inside it (up to four at once, by world
 *   position, adding no varying);
 * - sends the edit, a session write, and undoes the guess with the reason if
 *   the planet refuses it;
 * - hears the planet's changes in its WorldSync room, its own and others',
 *   and draws each changed chunk, and the far tiles over it, again. Where
 *   it hears nothing it draws the edited chunk again a little later, and
 *   again until the planet shows the change.
 * - edits at one block go one after another, each sent expecting what the
 *   one before left there; only the newest guess at a block shows.
 * - a bar of nine blocks (number keys and the wheel choose one) and a menu
 *   of every block offered (`e`), each the world's to hide.
 * Every outcome is told to the world's scripts as the action `planet.edit`.
 *
 * A visitor not signed in, on a planet that names its client at its issuer,
 * finds the bar covered by a notice; it opens the issuer's sign-in in a
 * popup (signIn.ts), whose return page on the planet's own origin hands the
 * code back to this tab, so the visitor stays in the world, where they
 * stood, and edits once it is done.
 *
 * A change the room tells is drawn once per version: a chunk told of many
 * times while it is being drawn is drawn once more after, not once each.
 *
 * The shaders are written in WGSL for WebGPU and GLSL for WebGL2, chosen by
 * the material's shader language, kept in step.
 */
